<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palma Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1229">1229</a>–<a href="/years/1601">1601</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Cathedral of Santa Maria of Palma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> Cathedral is the Gothic cathedral of Palma on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, begun after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a> conquered the island in 1229 and built mainly between about 1300 and 1601 on the site of the city's main mosque. Rising above the sea wall, it has one of the tallest Gothic naves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its interior was remodelled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoni-gaudi">Antoni Gaudí</a> in the early 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palma-cathedral.jpg" alt="Palma Cathedral" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kathedrale_von_Palma_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Cathedral of Santa Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a>, known as La Seu, stands on the seashore of Palma between the Royal Palace of La Almudaina and the Episcopal Palace. In 1229 King James I conquered Palma, then called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madina-mayurqa">Madina Mayurqa</a>, from its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> rulers. He at once re-established the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a>, and works began on the site of the city's largest mosque; Bishop Pere de Morella consecrated the site in 1230. The earliest surviving part is the Chapel of the Holy Trinity, finished in 1327 as a burial place for the kings of Mallorca. The last traces of the mosque disappeared in 1386, and the bell tower was finished in 1498, about when the naves and vaults were completed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The cathedral has three parallel naves with matching apses, which most scholars attribute to the influence of the cathedrals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-del-mar">Santa Maria del Mar</a>. It has no transept or ambulatory. It is 121 metres long and 40 metres wide, and its pillars are one twelfth the width of the vault, against one sixth at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. The great east rose window, nearly 14 metres across, is the second-largest Gothic rose window in the world after that of Strasbourg.</p><p>In the royal chapel at the end of the central nave stand the marble tombs of the Mallorcan kings James II and James III beside the white marble Episcopal Throne of 1346. Sixteen chapels line the nave and show nearly all the styles used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a> since the cathedral was begun, from the Plateresque Chapel of Sant Jeroni to the Churrigueresque Chapel of Our Lady of the Crown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Tridentine Council</a> the cathedral was richly decorated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> murals and altarpieces. In the middle of the 19th century structural problems led to a French-inspired neo-Gothic rebuilding of the west front, giving it its present gable. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoni-gaudi">Antoni Gaudí</a> was invited in 1903 to oversee a restoration and moved the choir stalls and screen from the centre of the nave to the sides of the presbytery. He designed a large canopy over the altar and opened bricked-up windows. His work has been criticised for destroying Mudejar and Baroque decoration, and it stopped in 1914 after disputes with the local authorities.</p><p>The most recent major works, from 2001 to 2006, were those of the Mallorcan artist Miquel Barceló in the Chapel of Sant Pere and the Holy Sacrament, where he covered the apse in Italian ceramic with scenes of the loaves and fishes and the wedding at Cana. The project drew strong criticism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palma Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
